3.1. Overview
                                                                       3.1.2. Current Challenges
3.1.1. Problem Formulation
                                                                       Anomaly Unawareness in CLIP: The CLIP-based AD
Zero-shot AD models are trained to identify anomalous                  method classifies visual features as “anomalies” if they ex-
samples whose categories may be unseen in the train-                   hibit greater similarities to anomaly prompt embeddings
ing dataset. Specifically, the model is expected to learn              than to normal prompt embeddings, thus requiring well-
                                                                       defined boundaries between these two kinds of prompts.
                                                                       However, in real applications, CLIP’s text embeddings of-
                                                                       ten lack the clear separability needed to reliably distinguish
                                                                       between normal and anomaly classes.

                                                                          We observe that, despite the visible defects in exam-
                                                                       ple images from the MVTec-AD [2], their features exhibit
                                                                       higher cosine similarity with “normal” prompts than with
                                                                       correct “anomaly” descriptions (see Fig. 2 (top)), indicating
                                                                       CLIP’s inaccurate semantic understanding. Without adap-

