Figure 6. Visualization of Anomaly Localization Results of                  5. Conclusion and Discussion
Original CLIP [42], AnomalyCLIP [59], VAND [7] and our
AA-CLIP. Compared to previous methods, AA-CLIP demon-                       To our knowledge, this is the first work to explicitly ana-
strates more reliable prediction capabilities in localizing anomaly.        lyze the intrinsic Anomaly Unawareness problem in CLIP.
                                                                            To tackle this issue, we propose a simple yet effective two-
anomaly-aware semantics. Line “4.” validates that, with                     stage training strategy to embed anomaly-aware informa-
AA-CLIP, the model’s ability to discriminate anomalies                      tion into CLIP, enabling clear disentanglement of anomaly
further improves, as the AA-CLIP’s text encoder provides                    representations across both seen and novel classes. By
a more precise semantic foundation. Adding Disentangle                      leveraging residual adapters, our method preserves CLIP’s
Loss leads to an additional improvement (shown in Line                      strong generalization ability, achieving outstanding zero-
“5.”), especially at image-level, validating the necessity of               shot performance across multiple datasets.
independence between normal and anomaly anchors. These
results underscore the crucial role of text space refinement                   Our adapted AA-CLIP, developed through this two-
in improved anomaly localization and classification.                        stage adaptation strategy, reveals the potential of refining
                                                                            CLIP’s feature space for improved performance in down-
Two-Stage Training: To validate the necessity of two-stage                  stream applications. Beyond addressing anomaly unaware-
training, we adapt both text and image encoders together                    ness, our work also provides a potential foundation for tack-
within one stage (also adopted by AdaCLIP). As shown                        ling other “unawareness” issues within CLIP. These may
                                                                            include limitations in context-awareness or specificity to
                                                                            domain-relevant nuances, suggesting further applications
                                                                            of our method in expanding CLIP’s adaptability across di-
                                                                            verse tasks. Additionally, we observe signs of overfitting
                                                                            with full-shot training, suggesting potential saturation dur-
                                                                            ing CLIP adaptation and warranting further investigation.

